---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import ProjectCard from "../components/ProjectCard.astro";
import { targets } from "../data/targets";

const featured = targets.filter((t) => t.featured);
---

<Base
  title="Exbound"
  description="Independent, compatible, open reimplementations of proprietary, abandoned, and monopolized scientific and engineering software."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Open scientific software reconstruction</p>
      <h1>The software your work depends on<br />shouldn't be able to disappear.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Exbound rebuilds proprietary, abandoned, and monopolized scientific and
        engineering software in the open — not just readers for the file
        formats, but working implementations of what the original did: open,
        edit, compute, fit, graph, script, save, and run.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/projects/">Explore the targets</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/contribute/">Contribute</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why this matters</p>
      <h2>Lock-in is a preservation problem</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Instruments outlive their software. Licenses lapse, vendors
          discontinue products, formats stay undocumented, and years of
          measurements become unreadable or uncomputable. A working instrument
          can be stranded by a dead application.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reading the files back is necessary but not sufficient. Exbound aims
          at the whole application — reproducing the computations, the
          workflows, and the behavior — verified against documented reference
          behavior, and described by compatibility rather than branding.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band-dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">How compatibility is established</p>
      <h2>Observe, implement, verify</h2>
      <div class="method-steps">
        <div class="method-step">
          <p class="n">01</p>
          <h3>Observe</h3>
          <p>
            Record what the reference software does, from lawfully held copies —
            files, versions, and computed outputs with the inputs that produced
            them.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="method-step">
          <p class="n">02</p>
          <h3>Implement</h3>
          <p>
            Rebuild the behavior independently and clean-room against that
            documented reference — formats, numerical methods, and workflows.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="method-step">
          <p class="n">03</p>
          <h3>Verify</h3>
          <p>
            Check the implementation against the observed behavior, and report
            coverage as observed / implemented / verified — never a guess.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Pilot &amp; first targets</p>
      <h2>Where the work starts</h2>
      <div class="card-grid">
        {featured.map((target) => <ProjectCard target={target} />)}
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/projects/">See all twelve targets</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">How progress is measured</p>
      <h2>A capability model, not a percentage</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Every target is assessed across nine capabilities — acquisition, data,
          documents, computation, workflow, programmability, instrument
          integration, the user-facing application, and overall replacement
          coverage — each tracked as what has been <em>observed</em> in the
          reference,
          <em>implemented</em> independently, and <em>verified</em> against it.
          No fabricated completion figures.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="https://github.com/exbound/research/blob/main/docs/capability-model.md"
            >Read the capability model →</a
          >
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
